\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


A finite relational structure $D$ consists of a finite set and specified relations. A homomorphism preserves every relation; the product $D^4$ has coordinatewise relations. A Borel CSP instance has a standard Borel space of variables and Borel relations of the prescribed signature; a solution is a Borel homomorphism into $D$. The notation $\operatorname{CSP}_B(D)$ denotes the source decision problem for coded instances. A $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^1_2$ set is a projection of a coanalytic set; completeness uses the coding and reductions of the cited descriptive-set-theoretic formulation. The displayed identity is required for every $a,r,e\in D$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For a finite relational structure $D$, absence of a homomorphism $f:D^4\to D$ satisfying $f(a,r,e,a)=f(r,a,r,e)$ makes $\operatorname{CSP}_B(D)$ $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}^1_2$-complete. Is the converse true? Can Borel complexity, efficient local solvability for directed graphs, and extension to all locally checkable labelings be characterized algebraically? \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2022/8}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
The first sentence records the supplied antecedent theorem, rather than claiming it is an open conjecture. The converse is the definite question; the remaining characterization directions retain the source-specific graph and reduction conventions.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record open. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does the absence of the stated four-variable algebraic operation exactly characterize the hardest Borel constraint problems, and can the same algebra explain local solvability?

